id: records/yawn.bot-state
title: "yawn.bot state"
kind: runtime-state
record_shape: header
status: active
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "scripts/render-bot-state.mjs, from the ledgers, decisions/, and core/entity-and-coupling.yawn; regenerate rather than edit"
updated: 2026-09-12
generated_by: scripts/render-bot-state.mjs

purpose: >
  One record a surface can read to show the bot's state truthfully. Every line
  names where it came from. The dropdown at yawn.bot/dave and the chat surface
  at yawn.bot/dave/chat read this record; they live in the runtime repository
  (agents/yawn.bot.yawn identity.repository) and are not wired from here.

runtime_ref: agents/yawn.bot.yawn
relationship: "yawn.bot/dave"
relationship_ref: core/entity-and-coupling.yawn

loop_status: awaiting_choice
loop_status_vocabulary: schemas/statuses.v1.schema.json#loopStatus
loop_status_reason: >
  52 decision records in decisions/ are proposed and none has a
  selected_by. The loop is at the choice step and the choice is Dave's. This is
  the protocol position; it says nothing about whether any scheduled automation
  is running.

observed_activity:
  last_observed_run: 2026-07-04
  observed_from: records/automation-log.yawn
  as_of: 2026-09-12
  epistemic_status: observed
  note: >
    The newest timestamp in any bot ledger in this repository. The automation
    contracts under automation/ describe daily and hourly schedules; nothing in
    this tree shows a run after this date. Do not display "sleeping" or "active"
    from the contracts; display the last observed run and its age.

principal_role_settled:
  question_key: role
  answer: "steward of yawn.bot"
  why: >
    So that Dave stays flexible and agile with as much agency as possible, while
    the bills are paid and leverage is as high as it can be.
  settled_on: 2026-09-12
  source: core/entity-and-coupling.yawn
  recall_rule: >
    When the role question resurfaces, answer from this record first — "last
    time we checked, what you are to be is steward of yawn.bot, so that…" — and do not
    reopen it unless Dave corrects the answer. It has dropped from the top slot.

next_question:
  decision_ref: decisions/001-one-canon-per-concept.yawn
  title: "One governing record per concept, lenses marked"
  question: >
    When two records define the same thing (lacuna, the loop, the objective
    lifecycle, a promotion gate, a read order), does one of them govern, and
    does it say so?
  leverage: 57
  leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved x split_weight(genuinely-split 1.0 | leaning 0.7 | lone-exception 0.4) x authored_conflict_bonus(both sides authored 1.5 | one side 1.2 | neither 1.0)"
  rule: >
    The highest-leverage proposed decision whose choice is still empty. A
    surface that opens on a new entry shows this one question. Rank is not
    importance, truth, obligation, or permission (core/inquiry-selection.yawn).

question_queue:
  - rank: 1
    decision_ref: decisions/001-one-canon-per-concept.yawn
    leverage: 57
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "When two records define the same thing (lacuna, the loop, the objective lifecycle, a promotion gate, a read order), does one of them govern, and does it say so?"
  - rank: 2
    decision_ref: decisions/002-coverage-allocation.yawn
    leverage: 53.8
    split: leaning
    question: "Should every record, loop and receipt get the same depth of proof, memory and indexing, or should the heavy machinery be concentrated on the few that can spend, publish or mutate, and should that choice be written down?"
  - rank: 3
    decision_ref: decisions/003-rules-that-can-fail.yawn
    leverage: 39
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "When the repo writes a rule down, must something be able to fail if the rule is broken, or is the written record itself the authority and compliance a matter of reading?"
  - rank: 4
    decision_ref: decisions/004-when-a-concern-earns-a-record.yawn
    leverage: 36
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "When does one concern earn its own .yawn — whenever it has a name or an ID, or only when its proof, owner, status or rollback would differ from its parent's?"
  - rank: 5
    decision_ref: decisions/005-field-obligation.yawn
    leverage: 34.8
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "When a record leaves a field off, does that mean the field was optional or that the record is incomplete, and should every contract say which fields are which?"
  - rank: 6
    decision_ref: decisions/006-runtime-dependence.yawn
    leverage: 26.4
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "Must a .yawn stay readable and checkable with nothing installed, or is it enough that the record reads with nothing while checking it needs Node and ajv and running it needs Dave's Windows desktop — and is that three-way split written anywhere?"
  - rank: 7
    decision_ref: decisions/007-closed-until-opened.yawn
    leverage: 26
    split: leaning
    question: "When a record, template or gate says nothing, is the answer no until someone opens it — and does that rule also cover the values a template ships prefilled, like reversible: true, status: accepted, or a consent list that starts with yes?"
  - rank: 8
    decision_ref: decisions/008-public-repo-privacy.yawn
    leverage: 25.2
    split: leaning
    question: "Is this public repository a public surface that must carry sanitized summaries only, or a source record that keeps full detail, including local file paths and real family names?"
  - rank: 9
    decision_ref: decisions/009-audience-primacy.yawn
    leverage: 24
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "Is 'human-readable first, machine-addressable second' a rule about how a .yawn reads and what leads it, or also about where proof effort and currency go — and if only the first, does any record say so?"
  - rank: 10
    decision_ref: decisions/010-list-order-semantics.yawn
    leverage: 24
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "When a record lists things, does the order mean anything, and if only sometimes, how does a reader or a bot know which lists are ranked?"
  - rank: 11
    decision_ref: decisions/011-label-first-prove-before-promote.yawn
    leverage: 24
    split: genuinely-split
    question: "May a record be marked active or accepted, or be cited as another record's proof, before its own evidence exists — so long as it is labelled — or must the proof land first?"
  - rank: 12
    decision_ref: decisions/012-shared-key-vocabulary.yawn
    leverage: 23.5
    split: leaning
    question: "Should there be one shared set of key names for what an agent may and may not do, for where a record sits in the loop, and for what a colour means, or may each record coin its own?"

held:
  - decision_ref: decisions/053-container-intelligence-name.yawn
    reason: "held by core/canonical-extension.yawn creation_gate.on_ambiguity; not ranked"

backlog:
  proposed: 52
  held: 1
  ratified: 0
  inventory_ref: JUDGMENTS.md

boundary:
  - "this record describes the bot; it is never a diagnosis of the person on the other side of the slash"
  - "a state shown from the contracts instead of the ledgers is a claim without proof"
  - "the bot cannot move this record past awaiting_choice; only a filled choice: in decisions/ can"

proof:
  covers:
    - observed_activity
    - next_question
    - question_queue
    - backlog
  condition: >
    node scripts/render-bot-state.mjs --check exits 0: the committed record
    equals what the ledgers, decisions/, and the settled role produce today.
  falsifier: >
    A surface shows a bot status this record does not carry, or next_question
    names a decision whose choice is already filled.
  status: contract-defined
